      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'A concave lens is a transparent optical lens that is thinner at the centre
    and thicker at the edges. It is also called a diverging lens because it bends
    parallel rays of light outward so that they appear to come from a point called
    the principal focus. The straight line passing through the optical centre and
    the two centres of curvature is called the principal axis.


    The image formed
    by a concave lens is generally virtual, upright, and diminished. When an object
    is placed in front of the lens, the refracted rays diverge and their backward
    extensions meet on the same side of the lens as the object. The image is formed
    between the principal focus and the optical centre.


    As the object moves
    farther away from the lens, the image becomes smaller and moves closer to the
    principal focus. When the object is very far away, the image is formed near the
    focus and is highly diminished. A concave lens does not normally form a real
    image on a screen when used with a real object.


    Concave lenses are used
    in spectacles for correcting short-sightedness (myopia), door peepholes, telescopes,
    binoculars, cameras, and other optical systems where light rays need to be diverged.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
